## What Problem This Solves

Dependency-heavy external plugins could block Gateway HTTP requests during startup and reload. Failed replacement could also leave Windows recovery captures pointing into a retired directory.

## User Impact

Gateway health requests remain responsive within the existing 15-second probe deadline in the tested installed workload. Syntax-invalid and registration-failing replacements are rejected while the last working plugin remains callable, and subsequent healthy replacements succeed.

Startup is still slow: 120.539 seconds in this run, with multi-second response delays. This fixes the measured availability regression; it is not a fast-startup or universal latency claim. No configuration changes are required.

## Why This Change Was Made

Capture runs in the existing retained preparation worker, with source custody transferred to the plugin instance and module-resolution hooks kept local. Host receipt validation processes 32 ordered events per batch and yields between batches; the synchronous CLI drains the same validation steps synchronously. Windows recovery links relocate into the new capture.

Existing instance/registry ownership, retirement checks, byte hashes, containment checks, native admission, publisher context, and joined shutdown remain intact. Root handles close before yielding. Main's selected-root ownership and receipt scoping (#176280, b81e502d2dd5498e235cad1a67049d845ce17317) are preserved.

This revision also addresses ClawSweeper's two P2 findings: the recovery test now injects failure at the actual source-preparation owner, and the unused remote-artifact mode/callback protocol is removed. Native-load tracking remains connected to its existing owner. No plugin-specific production directories are hardcoded.

