## What Problem This Solves

Screenshot diagnostics can stall the iOS release lane while synchronously collecting resource snapshots. In [the startup investigation](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/39460393891), roughly 40 seconds elapsed after the boot phase's recorded duration and before the next phase started; the resource probes run in that tail.

## User Impact

Screenshot phases no longer wait for duplicate native resource probes. Phase timing, simulator counts, sanitized startup/crash facts, cleanup, and the original capture failure remain available. This does not change the app, screenshot contents, release admission, or upload behavior.

## Why This Change Was Made

The existing background timeline already owns resource sampling. Remove the synchronous sampler and its obsolete fixture output, and use the background collector in ordinary CI screenshot capture as well. Retain the timeline in the existing diagnostics artifact. There is no additional sampler, retry, or simulator service change.

## Evidence

- 23 focused Fastlane/collector tests pass. The changed Fastlane suite takes 0.636 seconds of test time; both files complete in 3.48 seconds including wrapper startup. The updated CI planning contract also passes (0.14 seconds test time; 2.52 seconds including wrapper startup). Hosted CI timing is pending.
- Ruby syntax, targeted lint, formatting, workflow validation (actionlint, zizmor, and workflow guards), and `git diff --check` pass.
- Fresh independent review: scoped-clean through P2.
- Full nonpublishing native screenshot capture and evidence verification pass in final-head PR CI; broader CI is still waiting on shared runner assignment. The separate manual screenshot-only run has an unrelated Watch runtime failure, detailed below.

### Acceptance rule recorded before the performance comparison

Replay the committed startup/crash fixture from `ios-release-fastlane-gates.test.ts` against the baseline and candidate diagnostic owners, using five serial A/B pairs of fresh Ruby processes on the same macOS host with a warm OS. Count actual native resource launches, record host load, and report elapsed/CPU median, nearest-rank p95 (the maximum of five), and range. Native calls are not mocked. The expected count changes from eight to zero for initialization plus one measured phase; retained startup/crash facts and the original error must match in every sample. Investigate any 20% median/p95 timing or CPU regression before accepting the change. These owner-level measurements do not establish a release-duration percentile or reproduce cold simulator starvation.

For integration, run the full nonpublishing screenshot lane once on this candidate with the background timeline enabled: all nine iPhone/iPad/Watch screenshots must succeed, simulator cleanup must complete, and phase diagnostics plus the resource timeline must be retained. No App Store upload is part of this proof.

### Diagnostic-owner comparison

Baseline: `765ae71d6a6805ebf10d79e352e23c572d8f124b`; candidate and identical committed fixture: `eb9f9e8c3fc878629e88d48a5867acc364150b8d`. Five A/B pairs passed the original-error, startup, crash, privacy, and stale-report checks. Local host: macOS arm64, 10 logical CPUs, 32 GiB RAM, Ruby 4.0.5. Host load (1/5/15 minutes) was 2.69/2.54/2.20 throughout this short replay.

| Metric per fixture | Baseline median / p95 / range | Candidate median / p95 / range |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Actual native resource launches | 8 / 8 / 8–8 | 0 / 0 / 0–0 |
| Fresh Ruby fixture wall time | 139.98 / 145.11 / 136.76–145.11 ms | 49.68 / 56.38 / 47.59–56.38 ms |
| Ruby CPU after loading measurement prerequisites | 21.38 / 21.92 / 20.64–21.92 ms | 8.68 / 9.38 / 8.31–9.38 ms |
| Child-process CPU | 75.50 / 79.33 / 74.44–79.33 ms | 0 / 0 / 0–0 ms |

The count rule passes, and neither measured timing/CPU metric regressed. This is an owner-level work-deletion result, not an estimate of hosted release speedup. The script below is measurement orchestration; the workload itself is the committed fixture, unchanged between arms. The diff removes 43 production Ruby lines and 19 net test lines; the workflow/docs additions preserve collection and describe the single sampler.

<details>
<summary>Reproduce the diagnostic-owner comparison</summary>

Save the following driver as `/tmp/diagnostic-proof.mjs`, then run from this candidate checkout:

```sh
git show 765ae71d6a6805ebf10d79e352e23c572d8f124b:apps/ios/fastlane/screenshot_diagnostics.rb > /tmp/baseline-screenshot-diagnostics.rb
node /tmp/diagnostic-proof.mjs test/scripts/ios-release-fastlane-gates.test.ts /tmp/baseline-screenshot-diagnostics.rb "$PWD/apps/ios/fastlane/screenshot_diagnostics.rb"
```

```js
import assert from 'node:assert/strict';
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import { loadavg } from 'node:os';
import { spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';
import { runInNewContext } from 'node:vm';
const [fixturePath, baseline, candidate] = process.argv.slice(2);
const text = readFileSync(fixturePath, 'utf8');
const start = text.indexOf('it("saves bounded startup and crash facts');
assert(start >= 0);
const literal = text.slice(start).match(/const source = (`[\s\S]*?`);/)[1];
assert(!literal.includes('${'));
const fixture = runInNewContext(literal, Object.create(null), { timeout: 1000 });
const prelude = `
require "json"
require "open3"
$proof_calls = 0
module NativeProof
  def popen2(*arguments, **options, &block)
    $proof_calls += 1
    super
  end
end
Open3.singleton_class.prepend(NativeProof)
$proof_cpu = Process.times
at_exit do
  done = Process.times
  warn "OPENCLAW_DIAGNOSTICS_PROOF:" + JSON.generate({
    calls: $proof_calls,
    selfCpuMs: 1000 * (done.utime + done.stime - $proof_cpu.utime - $proof_cpu.stime),
    childCpuMs: 1000 * (done.cutime + done.cstime - $proof_cpu.cutime - $proof_cpu.cstime)
  })
end
`;
const records = [];
for (let pair = 1; pair <= 5; pair++) {
  for (const [variant, owner] of [['baseline', baseline], ['candidate', candidate]]) {
    const hostLoad = loadavg();
    const started = performance.now();
    const result = spawnSync('ruby', ['-e', prelude + fixture, owner], {
      encoding: 'utf8', timeout: 120000, maxBuffer: 1024 * 1024,
    });
    const elapsedMs = performance.now() - started;
    assert.equal(result.status, 0, result.stderr);
    assert(!result.stdout.includes('PRIVATE-'));
    const report = JSON.parse(result.stdout);
    assert.equal(report.error, 'original capture failed');
    assert.equal(report.events.length, 1);
    const event = report.events[0];
    assert.equal(event.phase, 'capture');
    assert.equal(event.outcome, 'failed');
    assert.deepEqual(event.startup, { testCaseStarts: 0, instrumentsConnectionTimeout: true, runnerBootstrapFailure: true, stalledWait: true });
    assert.deepEqual(event.crashes, [{ process: 'OpenClawUITests-Runner', exception: { type: 'EXC_CRASH', signal: 'SIGABRT' }, termination: { namespace: 'SIGNAL', code: 6 }, triggeredThreadFrames: [{ symbol: '-[XCTWaiter(StallHandling) handleStalledWait:]' }] }]);
    if (variant === 'baseline') {
      for (const sample of [event.before, event.after]) {
        assert(sample.hardware.logicalCpuCount > 0);
        assert(sample.memoryPageBytes > 0);
        assert.equal(sample.loadAverage.length, 3);
      }
    }
    const line = result.stderr.split('\n').find(line => line.startsWith('OPENCLAW_DIAGNOSTICS_PROOF:'));
    assert(line);
    const counters = JSON.parse(line.slice('OPENCLAW_DIAGNOSTICS_PROOF:'.length));
    assert.equal(counters.calls, variant === 'baseline' ? 8 : 0);
    records.push({ pair, variant, hostLoad, elapsedMs, ...counters });
  }
}
const summary = {};
for (const variant of ['baseline', 'candidate']) {
  summary[variant] = {};
  for (const metric of ['calls', 'elapsedMs', 'selfCpuMs', 'childCpuMs']) {
    const values = records.filter(row => row.variant === variant).map(row => row[metric]).sort((a,b) => a-b);
    summary[variant][metric] = { median: values[2], p95: values[4], min: values[0], max: values[4] };
  }
}
console.log(JSON.stringify({ scope: 'real diagnostic failure fixture; fresh Ruby process; warm host OS', records, summary }, null, 2));

```

</details>

The follow-up commit `c8e81f9b256b733f25ecf412705bac0619c845bb` only updates workflow test expectations; the measured Ruby owner, replay fixture, collector, screenshot lane, and workflows are unchanged. Hosted screenshot proof is running on `eb9f9e8c3fc878629e88d48a5867acc364150b8d`; PR CI covers the final head.

### Hosted screenshot evidence

The final-head [PR CI screenshot job](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/39494811770/job/118878428180) and evidence reducer passed. They tested GitHub's PR merge revision `b86dc4bb78f6b7f6544ead481ea661075268631b` for head `c8e81f9b256b733f25ecf412705bac0619c845bb` on `xcode-27-xlarge` (5 CPUs, 14 GiB; Xcode 27A266a).

- All nine iPhone/iPad/Watch PNGs are present; all eight XCTest captures passed on attempt one, and Watch capture succeeded.
- All 24 phases succeeded. Each of the three task-owned simulators was deleted with zero active simulators afterward. Phase timings, simulator counts, and startup flags remain; synchronous resource snapshots are absent.
- Boot-to-next-phase nominal gaps were 0.34 / 0.24 / -0.17 seconds (timestamps have one-second precision). The previously observed multi-second diagnostic tail is absent in this run.
- The background timeline has 40 samples, a successful exit footer, and 557,027 bytes. Its Node-only CPU time was 0.310 seconds over 740.568 seconds of workload; peak recorded Node RSS was 62.4 MiB. Native observation CPU is excluded. Nine unavailable probes remain explicitly recorded rather than blocking capture.
- [Screenshot evidence artifact](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/39494811770/artifacts/12298512460), [phase and resource diagnostics artifact](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/39494811770/artifacts/12298801799).

The separate [manual screenshot-only run](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/39494550226) passed all eight iPhone/iPad captures and cleaned up both simulators, then failed before Watch creation with `No available watchOS runtime`. Its log advertised watchOS 27.0 at startup but no available runtime at Watch selection. That runtime preparation/resolution code is unchanged by this branch; PR CI uses stronger boot-probed runtime preparation and passed the same complete screenshot lane. The manual run's failure remains unresolved and is not counted as a full success. Its diagnostics were successfully retained in [the partial-run artifact](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/39494550226/artifacts/12297763232).

This satisfies the complete nonpublishing lane proof through PR CI, while leaving the manual runtime-availability issue as a separate follow-up. It does not establish a release-duration percentile or resolve all runner starvation.

### Remaining CI blocker

After native capture and evidence verification passed, [the final-head CI run](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/39494811770) had 39 jobs queued without assigned runners for over 20 minutes, with no jobs running and no failures. The existing 4/8/16-class pools are also unassigned in unrelated [run 39495163993](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/39495163993) and [run 39495106724](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/39495106724). Runner labels, dependencies, concurrency, and admission are unchanged by this PR. The available data establishes a shared runner-assignment delay, not its provider-side cause. No reruns, backend changes, or provisioning were performed. Broad CI remains pending; this is not an all-green claim.

